<p>Lebanese Prime Minister Nawaf Salam said on Sunday that he is working to end the Israel-Hezbollah war and secure the withdrawal of Israeli troops from southern Lebanon. But Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, speaking to troops in southern Lebanon, said the fighting was &quot;far from over&quot; and signaled that Israeli operations there would continue. At the same time, diplomacy is moving forward. Israeli and Lebanese officials are due to hold rare direct talks in Washington this week, according to The New York Times, even as Israel launched fresh attacks in Lebanon in the days before the meeting. That mix of renewed strikes and planned talks underscores both the immediate danger on the ground and a narrow opening for de-escalation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/txI_PPG6QgSjPx6-DYcBNLsqEnsWBhRZny34ksuhAvCrCtm2OPPNR9knNxQM0hiHI26GRBICC1eMiiQjZQ-4AfZy77d-_epADb5TmtTZ9ngsddVW5fsPpWF5h_hN-UEaBNukb8tmn8bSaiVysoKxm8Etq4mpHR8W4HEJ9eBBonW5HOwU5NnyI6sD8iXO0cqHtftoiLT2TGMg8xL9' alt='A building on Saturday in the Lebanese capital, Beirut, that was destroyed by Israeli strikes.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Red Cross loss: The Lebanese Red Cross said an Israeli drone &quot;directly targeted&quot; an ambulance team on a humanitarian mission, killing one paramedic despite the clearly displayed Red Cross emblem.</li><li>Israeli rationale: Netanyahu said Israel had &quot;thwarted the threat of invasion from Lebanon,&quot; casting the continued campaign as a security measure against Hezbollah.</li><li>Ceasefire tension: Israel has said the ceasefire tied to the Iran war does not cover its conflict with Hezbollah, leaving the Lebanese front as a point of strain around the broader U.S.-Iran truce.</li></ul><blockquote>War continues - Benjamin Netanyahu</blockquote><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/lebanon-pm-says-hes-working-to-get-israeli-troops-to-withdraw'>Lebanon PM says he’s working to get Israeli troops t